==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fr|en}} {{m|fr|galvaniser}}, from {{m|fr|galvanisme}}, after Italian {{l|en|physiologist}} {{w|Luigi Galvani|Luigi Aloisio Galvani}} (1737&ndash;1798).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈɡælvənaɪ̯z/|lang=en}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|galvanise}} {{qualifier|mostly UK}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|galvaniz}}

# {{label|en|transitive|chiefly|_|North America|chemistry}} To coat with a thin layer {{l|en|of [[metal]] by [[electrochemical]] means; to [[electroplate]].}}
# {{label|en|transitive|chiefly|_|North America}} To coat with rust-resistant {{l|en|zinc}}.
# {{label|en|transitive|chiefly|_|North America}} To {{l|en|[[shock]] or [[stimulate]]}} into sudden activity.
# {{label|en|transitive|archaic}} To {{l|en|electrify}}.
#* {{w|Thomas Babington Macaulay}}
#*: The agitations resembled the grinnings and writhings of a '''galvanized''' corpse, not the struggles of an athletic man.
# {{label|en|transitive|dated|American Civil War}} To switch sides between {{l|en|[[Union]] and [[Confederate]]}}.
#* {{quote-book|title={{w|Confederates in the Attic}}|author={{w|Tony Horwitz}}|year=1998|edition=1st Vintage Departures|publisher=Vintage Books|isbn=0679439781|page=10|passage=Reeanactors called this "'''galvanizing'''," the Civil War term for soldiers who switched sides during the conflict.}}


====Synonyms====
* {{sense|to shock or stimulate}} {{l|en|startle}}

